<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hudson Bay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hudson Bay is a large inland sea in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. It became the domain of the English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> from the late seventeenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> recognised British claims to the region in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> of 1713, an early gain in the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hudson-bay.jpg" alt="Hudson Bay" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hudson_Bay_2008-07-21_by_Aqua.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Utrecht (1713) brought the first large imperial gains: Gibraltar, Minorca, Nova Scotia, Newfoundland, the <b>Hudson Bay</b> territories and the asiento (the contract to supply enslaved Africans to Spanish America).</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Utrecht of 1713 had already surrendered Acadia, Newfoundland and the <b>Hudson Bay</b> region to Britain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>He excavated sites on Baffin Island and around north-western <b>Hudson Bay</b> and argued, correctly, that they were the remains of a whaling culture that had begun in Alaska and moved into Arctic Canada about a thousand years earlier.</blockquote><a href="/culture/thule-people">Thule people · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The company received a monopoly of trade across the lands draining into <b>Hudson Bay</b>, a vast territory named Rupert's Land.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-2">2 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cree and the Assiniboine, or Nakoda, had long been allies, and in the 18th century they became the main middlemen between the Hudson's Bay Company posts on <b>Hudson Bay</b> and peoples of the interior.</blockquote><a href="/state/iron-confederacy">Iron Confederacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1670 Charles II chartered the Hudson's Bay Company, giving it trading rights and ownership of all lands draining into <b>Hudson Bay</b>, named Rupert's Land after its first governor, Prince Rupert.</blockquote><a href="/state/ruperts-land">Rupert's Land</a></li></ul></section>
